Input: {"question": "What is the primary medium associated with the New York School?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0060-08b9403fcc39:d00", "start": 0, "end": 81, "text": "while their writing style was often described as cosmopolitan and world-traveled."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0060-08b9403fcc39:d00", "start": 82, "end": 217, "text": "The poets often wrote in an immediate and spontaneous manner reminiscent of stream of consciousness writing, often using vivid imagery."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0060-08b9403fcc39:d00", "start": 218, "end": 442, "text": "They drew on inspiration from Surrealism and the contemporary avant-garde art movements, in particular the action painting of their friends in the New York City art world circle such as Jackson Pollock and Willem de Kooning."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0060-08b9403fcc39:d00", "start": 443, "end": 672, "text": "Poets often associated with the New York School include John Ashbery, Frank O'Hara, Kenneth Koch, James Schuyler, Barbara Guest, Ted Berrigan, Bernadette Mayer, Alice Notley, Kenward Elmslie, Frank Lima, Ron Padgett, Lewis Warsh,"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0060-08b9403fcc39:d01", "start": 0, "end": 142, "text": "the first was centered around a core group of five poets : John Ashbery , Barbara Guest , James Schuyler , Kenneth Koch , and Frank O ’ Hara ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0060-08b9403fcc39:d01", "start": 143, "end": 258, "text": "The second generation included poets Alice Notley and her husband , Ted Berrigan ; Bill Berkson ; and Ron Padgett ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0060-08b9403fcc39:d01", "start": 259, "end": 400, "text": "During the second generation , members founded nonacademic learning centers that served local communities , such as the Poetry Project at St."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "